},{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"Vehicle and engine manufacturers would face different regulatory requirements depending on whether California's stricter emissions standards remain in effect or are preempted by federal rules."},{"industry":"construction","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Construction firms use off-road diesel equipment covered by the waiver, and the bill would affect compliance costs and equipment specifications for these machines."},{"industry":"agriculture","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"Agricultural operations use off-road diesel engines and heavy-duty vehicles subject to the emissions standards that would be affected by revoking the waiver."}],"plainSummary":"This joint resolution would cancel a waiver that allows California to enforce stricter emissions standards than federal rules for heavy-duty trucks, buses, and off-road diesel equipment starting with 2024 models. The waiver, granted by the Environmental Protection Agency under the Clean Air Act, permits California to set its own limits on nitrogen oxide pollution from these vehicles and engines. If passed, the resolution would restore federal preemption and prevent California from enforcing these particular state-level standards.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who drives. People living near a road, a plant or a port.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"W000806","score":0.05252904442813575},"overlapCount":32},"meaning":{"percentile":37.05631803123521,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.692529856991734}},{"billId":"119-hjres-87","bioguideId":"W000816","cycle":2026,"score":0.003662111515349859,"matches":[{"industry":"transport","donorAmount":13000,"donorShare":0.009617666605969327,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.00304559442522362},{"industry":"utilities-electric","donorAmount":5000,"donorShare":0.0036991025407574333,"billConfidence":0.5,"contribution":0.0006165170901262388}],"