"rationale":"Changes to zero-emission vehicle requirements could affect electricity demand and infrastructure planning for fleet electrification."}],"plainSummary":"This resolution would cancel waivers that allow California to enforce its own stricter emission standards for heavy-duty trucks and engines. Under the Clean Air Act, California can request permission to set its own vehicle pollution rules that are tougher than federal standards; this resolution would revoke several such waivers related to truck emissions, warranties, zero-emission airport shuttles, and advanced clean truck requirements. If passed, California would lose the authority to enforce these particular state-level vehicle pollution rules.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who drives. People living near a road, a plant or a port.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"L000566","score":0.030207281417461054},"overlapCount":24},"meaning":{"percentile":16.721880422779616,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.692529856991734}},{"billId":"119-hr-633","bioguideId":"W000816","cycle":2026,"score":0.002603072158310786,"matches":[{"industry":"tech","donorAmount":5000,"donorShare":0.0036991025407574333,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.002603072158310786}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.692529856991734},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-633","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"633","title":"TAKE IT DOWN Act","introducedDate":"2025-01-22","latestActionDate":"2025-04-28","latestActionText":"Placed on the Union Calendar, Calendar No. 59.","policyArea":"Science, Technology, Communications","subjects":["Child safety and welfare","Crimes against children","Criminal investigation, prosecution, interrogation","Digital media","Fraud offenses and financial crimes","Internet, web applications, social media","Pornography","Sex offenses"],"sponsorBioguideId":"S000168","cosponsorCount":40,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/633","industries":[{"industry":"tech","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly imposes new content-moderation obligations and 48-hour removal requirements on platforms that host user-generated content."},{"industry":"media-entertainment","confidence":0.4,"rationale":"Online publishers and digital media platforms may face compliance obligations if they host user-generated intimate content."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would make it a federal crime to publish intimate images or videos of someone online without their consent, including computer-generated deepfakes, when the publication is intended to cause harm or involves a minor. It would require websites and apps that host user-generated content to remove such images within 48 hours of being notified by the person depicted. Violators would face criminal penalties including prison time, fines, and mandatory restitution to victims.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Children, and the adults responsible for them. Anyone who uses the internet.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"O000019","score":0.06258174186191032},"overlapCount":39},"meaning":{"percentile":11.484461271493926,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.692529856991734}}],"votes":[{"id":"a64a90b759595d68f3a8d502","billId":"119-hr-7008","date":"2026-07-22T16:51:00-04:00","question":"Yea-and-Nay","result":"Passed","position":"Yea","sourceUrl":"https://clerk.house.gov/evs/2026/roll280.xml"},{"id":"2afa499bcaceefdcecfa862a","billId":"119-hr-8800","date":"2026-07-22T16:37:00-04:00","question":"Yea-and-Nay","result":"Passed","position":"Yea","sourceUrl":"https://cler